/CON
/0

/OBJ
;obj  starts  weight    c w  5 4 3 2 1 0 9 8 7 6 5 4 3 2 1 0    noun   adjective
/0    0           1     _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _    BENCH  _
/1    0           1     _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _    KEY    _
/2    0           5     _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _    CHEST  _

/PRO 0
; Entry 1: first boot. Sets the carry-proof witness flags (100, 101),
; the boot/return marker (102), places KEY into inventory, takes the
; RAMSAVE snapshot (with 103 still 0 - this is the ONLY RAMSAVE in
; either file, deliberately never refreshed again, so a later RAMLOAD
; anywhere always restores exactly this moment - the caveat-8 stale-
; slot behaviour, proven here by surviving three further switches),
; then attempts the switch to part 2. On success this entry never
; finishes - switch_to_part resets the stack and enters part 2's own
; PRO 0 fresh, so nothing after EXTERN 2 4 ever runs. "PA SWITCH F" and
; the fallthrough below run ONLY if the probe for GAME2.DDB failed:
; EXTERN is action-typed, so a failed switch just returns and execution
; continues with the next condact in THIS entry - the same established
; idiom as condacts.dsf check 44.
> _ _   EQ 102 0
        LET 100 77
        LET 101 88
        LET 102 1
        PLACE 1 254
        RAMSAVE
        MES "PA BOOT#n"
        XMESSAGE "part one external text"
        EXTERN 2 4
        MES "PA SWITCH F#n"

; Entry 2: RETURN-VIA-EXTERN. Reached when part 2 switches back here
; with EXTERN 1 4 - flag 103 is 1 on this path (part B set it live
; before switching back), distinguishing it from the RAMLOAD-restored
; return (entry 4) where 103 reads back 0 (the stale RAMSAVE value).
; LOAD 0 reads the save part B just wrote (part byte 2, curPart here is
; 1) - a CROSS-PART load, so a successful read auto-switches forward
; into part 2 and this entry never finishes either. "PA XLOAD F" is a
; separate, sibling-like entry (LOAD is condition-typed, so its own
; failure aborts THIS entry rather than falling through in place - see
; that entry's own gate for why it cannot be a bare fallthrough).
> _ _   EQ 102 1
        EQ 103 1
        MES "PA RETURN OK#n"
        MES "PA TYPE NAME: pt#n"
        LOAD 0

; Entry 3: LOAD-auto-switch failure witness. Gated on EQ 103 1 (not a
; bare wildcard): entry 2 never mutates flag 103, so this gate reliably
; tells "entry 2 ran and its LOAD failed" (103 still 1) apart from
; "entry 2's own gate already failed for an unrelated reason" (103
; would be 0, and this entry's gate then also correctly steps aside).
> _ _   EQ 103 1
        MES "PA XLOAD F#n"

; Entry 4: RETURN-VIA-RAMLOAD. Reached when part 2's RAMLOAD restores
; the part-1 buffer taken back in entry 1 - flag 103 reads 0 here
; because that RAMSAVE predates part B ever setting 103, proving the
; stale-slot snapshot survived two more switches (B's own EXTERN back,
; then this RAMLOAD) unchanged. This branch terminates the whole run.
> _ _   EQ 102 1
        EQ 103 0
        MES "PA RAMLOAD OK#n"
        END
/END
